lumina-lock (0.4.9-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed the lock screen's own entry. The clock and date used to show up
    small and fully opaque, then grow while apparently losing and regaining
    their opacity. Two independent causes, both removed:
    - The type size is now animated only once the scene has settled. `unit`
      is derived from the window height, which is 0 until the window has been
      sized, so the first layout animated the size up from nothing on every
      lock. Until sceneReady the size snaps instead of animating.
    - The glass text's opaque fallback no longer keys off `sampling`, which
      is also false for the first frame or two before the item has been laid
      out; that flashed an opaque white clock over the wallpaper until the
      glass replaced it. The fallback now appears only when the glass
      genuinely cannot be rendered, which is a persistent condition. The
      entry is a single 480 ms fade at a constant size.
  * The video wallpaper no longer cuts from its poster to the moving frame.
    The poster hid itself within a single frame of the video starting; it now
    cross-fades over 500 ms, so the still cover dissolves into the wallpaper.
    The poster's visibility follows its own opacity, which keeps it alive for
    the length of the fade.
  * Every fade now goes through the shared MotionBehavior: the clock's entry
    reveal, the static wallpaper's fade-in, the poster dissolve and the inline
    error each still carried their own Behaviour and their own curve.
    MotionBehavior takes an optional duration, so the curve lives in one place
    and only the length varies by use.

 -- Lumina <lumina@example.com>  Tue, 15 Sep 2026 19:20:00 +0800

lumina-lock (0.4.8-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Clock and date font size are configurable: 80-240 and 14-48, expressed in
    reference pixels at a 1080px-tall screen and scaled to the actual
    resolution, set from the control center with a spin box next to each
    weight row. The authenticating sizes stay a fixed fraction of the idle
    ones, so a custom size keeps the same relationship between the two states.
    Bounds are enforced in C++ so a hand-edited config cannot shrink the clock
    into illegibility or push the date off the screen.
  * Retuned the transition curve. Every state-transition animation now shares
    one curve, a standard ease-in-out cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1), replacing
    Easing.OutCubic: OutCubic spent ~58% of the motion in the first quarter of
    the time, which reads as "jump, then creep" when a large element both moves
    and resizes. Measured on the scene dim, the new curve is at 8% of the
    change after 34 ms and 40% after 118 ms, where OutCubic would be at 28% and
    75%. The curve is declared once in Theme and applied through a shared
    MotionBehavior component, so the call sites cannot drift apart again.
    Note for future edits: the curve must be set through `easing.bezierCurve`,
    not `easing.type` — the type setter builds a fresh QEasingCurve and would
    discard the control points.

